ABSTRACT: Autonomous transportation systems face a critical challenge in handling 'long tail events,' which are rare and unpredictable scenarios that are underrepresented in training data and difficult to anticipate during testing. Unlike human operators, who can flexibly adapt to novel situations, current autonomous systems lack the contextual awareness to respond effectively. To address this limitation, this paper presents the use of Large Language Model (LLM) and Vision-Language Model (VLM)-based anomaly detection and notification system integrated into an existing autonomous electric golf-cart platform developed at James Madison University. Building on prior work in autonomous vehicle development, the proposed system continuously monitors internal system state, user interactions, and visual data from exterior and interior cameras to detect dangerous or unusual situations and relay concise alerts to a remote human operator.This work further investigates the trade-offs between on-vehicle and cloud-based inference pipelines, evaluating key metrics such as latency, robustness, cybersecurity exposure, and privacy risk. Adversarial threats, including telemetry spoofing and video input manipulation, are examined, and corresponding defenses are proposed. The project culminates in the development of a secure, functional prototype alongside a planned public dataset release designed to benchmark safety detection performance and false-alarm rates, fostering reproducible research across the autonomous systems community. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0181] year=2026 | venue=Abdominal Radiology | type=Article
TITLE: Large language models and prostate MRI reporting: a stringent testbed for safe deployment under evolving AI, health-data, and cybersecurity regulation
KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence; Clinical decision support; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; European health data space; Government regulation; Health communication; Language models; Magnetic resonance imaging; PI-RADS; Prostatic neoplasms
ABSTRACT: Prostate magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) reporting is a high-impact communication task because small differences in lesion laterality, sector localization, lesion size, Prostate Imaging-Reporting and Data System (PI-RADS) categorization, or staging language can change biopsy targeting, surveillance, counseling, and treatment planning. At the same time, widespread patient-portal access means that many patients encounter radiology reports before clinical discussion and may seek explanations from public large language models (LLMs). LLMs can restructure radiology text, extract discrete variables, draft supervised summaries, and generate patient-facing explanations; however, fluency does not establish factual correctness. Hallucinated measurements, omitted qualifiers, flipped negations, wrong laterality, and overconfident statements about cancer likelihood create patient-safety, privacy, cybersecurity, and accountability risks, particularly when reports are copied into systems outside clinical governance. International regulation is also evolving rather than settled. In the European Union (EU), the Artificial Intelligence Act establishes a risk-based framework, but ongoing implementation activity–including the Digital Omnibus and related AI Act amendment proposals, draft guidance for high-risk AI systems, and the European Health Data Space (EHDS) Regulation–shows that practical rules for health-data access, cybersecurity, incident reporting, human oversight, logging, and liability still require local operationalization. In the United States (U.S.), Food and Drug Administration (FDA) clinical decision support (CDS) guidance clarifies boundaries relevant to radiology language-model workflows and emphasizes that health care professionals must be able to independently review the basis for recommendations. Against this backdrop, this Perspective uses prostate MRI as a stringent testbed and proposes a conservative roadmap that prioritizes bounded, auditable tasks–structured extraction, completeness checks, quality-assurance support, and source-linked patient addenda–over autonomous classification or unsupervised counseling. Safe adoption will depend on “no new facts” generation, provenance, human sign-off, local validation, access controls, incident-response planning, and continuous post-deployment monitoring. © The Author(s) 2026.

[R0182] year=2025 | venue=ACIT 2025 - 26th International Arab Conference on Information Technology, Conference Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: CyberPentest: An Automated Penetration Testing Framework Powered by LLMs
KEYWORDS: Cybersecurity; Exploit; Hallucinations; LLMs; Penetration testing; RAG (retrieval-Augmented generation); Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Distributed computer systems; Environmental testing; Network security; Cyber security; Exploit; Hallucination; High costs; Language model; Large language model; Manual methods; Penetration testing; Retrieval-augmented generation; Testing framework; Automation
ABSTRACT: Cybersecurity teams face increasing difficulty in conducting timely and comprehensive penetration testing due to the high cost, expertise requirements, and inconsistent outcomes of manual methods. Automated approaches, while promising, often suffer from inflexible execution and inaccuracies, especially when powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) that are prone to hallucinations. This paper presents CyberPentest, an AI-driven automated penetration testing framework that integrates LLMs with a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mechanism to minimize hallucinations and improve test reliability. The system emulates essential penetration testing tasks, including reconnaissance, vulnerability scanning, and exploit simulation, within a controlled environment. Our implementation, supported by a Django-based web interface, demonstrates higher accuracy and reliability compared to existing LLM-based penetration testing solutions. Beyond its technical contributions, this work reflects our journey as cybersecurity students, transitioning from a theoretical understanding to practical system development. By combining domain knowledge with collaborative experimentation, we contribute a validated methodology for secure, scalable, and explainable AI-driven penetration testing. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0183] year=2026 | venue=Procedia Computer Science | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Absolute Zero Reasoning for Autonomous Penetration Testing: A Hybrid LLM-MCTS Framework with Action-Constrained Exploration
KEYWORDS: action masking; autonomous penetration testing; CyberBattleSim; large language models; Monte Carlo Tree Search; simulation-grounded verification; Autonomous agents; Chains; Computer simulation languages; Forestry; Memory architecture; Monte Carlo methods; Network security; Absolute zero; Action masking; Autonomous penetration testing; Cyberbattlesim; Language model; Large language model; Monte carlo tree search; Penetration testing; Simulation-grounded verification; Tree-search; Computational linguistics
ABSTRACT: Automated security validation in enterprise networks requires agents that can reason over large combinatorial action spaces without falling into repetitive exploit loops. We present a hybrid architecture that combines Qwen-1.5B as a probabilistic action-prior generator with Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) as a simulation-based verification layer. Before executing any action, the agent runs 100 internal MCTS rollouts guided by a PUCT selection rule; failed actions are suppressed via dynamic action masking, forcing the search toward alternative attack vectors. Evaluation on the Chain-10 scenario in Microsoft CyberBattleSim shows that the hybrid agent achieves Local Privilege Escalation (LPE) in the episodes where valid exploit chains exist, with an average Steps to First Discovery of 1.67, substantially fewer steps than a standalone LLM baseline that exhibits persistent hallucination loops. The aggregate success rate across all episodes is 6.67%; therefore, we interpret the result as a controlled proof-of-concept of successful exploit-chain verification rather than as a statistically generalizable performance estimate. These results support the principle that combining language-model intuition with deterministic search verification can improve stability over standalone LLM action selection in a controlled cyber-simulation setting. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.. All rights reserved.

[R0184] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Future Machine Learning and Data Science, FMLDS 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: LIMA: Leveraging Large Language Models and MCP Servers for Initial Machine Access
KEYWORDS: AI-driven Cybersecurity; Cybersecurity Reconnaissance; Initial Access; Large Language Models; Prompt Engineering; Artificial intelligence; Cybersecurity; Graphical user interfaces; Network security; AI-driven cybersecurity; Cyber security; Cybersecurity reconnaissance; Initial access; Language model; Large language model; Model contexts; Penetration testing; Prompt engineering; Cost benefit analysis
ABSTRACT: We present LIMA (Large Language-Model-driven Initial Machine Access), a penetration testing framework that pairs off-the-shelf LLMs with Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers to orchestrate automated initial-access reconnaissance, enumeration, and exploitation. LIMA's modular design allows an LLM to invoke scanners, parse outputs, consult vulnerability databases, and generate exploits while receiving minimal human input. We evaluate Claude 3.5 and GPT-4o on five targets: four HackTheBox machines and a custom VM and compare their performance with that of beginner- and expert-level human testers. Claude finished four boxes in a mean time of 13 minutes which is up to 2x faster than the expert and required help only for CAPTCHAs, achieving autonomous completion otherwise. GPT-4o, with sporadic guidance, completed two boxes in 17.5 minutes. Both models failed on a BurpSuite-dependent target, showing that missing GUI tooling, not model logic, is now the main bottleneck. Token-cost analysis puts a full autonomous run at ≤ 0.05. These results establish the first quantitative baseline for AI-augmented penetration testing at the initial-access phase. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0185] year=2023 | venue=International Symposium on Empirical Software Engineering and Measurement | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Evaluating the Impact of ChatGPT on Exercises of a Software Security Course
KEYWORDS: artificial intelligence; ChatGPT; IT education; large language models; Software security; Application programs; Computational linguistics; Curricula; Education computing; Engineering education; Technology transfer; ChatGPT; IT-education; Language model; Large language model; Security Practice; Security-aware; Software security; Sources of informations; WEB application; Web applications; Students
ABSTRACT: Along with the development of large language models (LLMs), e.g., ChatGPT, many existing approaches and tools for software security are changing. It is, therefore, essential to understand how security-aware these models are and how these models impact software security practices and education. In exercises of a software security course at our university, we ask students to identify and fix vulnerabilities we insert in a web application using state-of-the-art tools. After ChatGPT, especially the GPT-4 version of the model, we want to know how the students can possibly use ChatGPT to complete the exercise tasks. We input the vulnerable code to ChatGPT and measure its accuracy in vulnerability identification and fixing. In addition, we investigated whether ChatGPT can provide a proper source of information to support its outputs. Results show that ChatGPT can identify 20 of the 28 vulnerabilities we inserted in the web application in a white-box setting, reported three false positives, and found four extra vulnerabilities beyond the ones we inserted. ChatGPT makes nine satisfactory penetration testing and fixing recommendations for the ten vulnerabilities we want students to fix and can often point to related sources of information. © 2023 IEEE.

