TITLE: LASR: A Novel Framework for Industrial Information Security Range Based on Large Models
KEYWORDS: attack detection; cybersecurity; hypergraph simulation; Industrial Internet; large models; lightweight ranges; traffic generation; Complex networks; Computer simulation languages; Cybersecurity; Industrial management; Intelligent agents; Intelligent systems; Modeling languages; Multi agent systems; Network security; Semantics; Topology; Attack detection; Cyber security; Hyper graph; Hypergraph simulation; Industrial internet; Language model; Large models; Lightweight range; Range-based; Traffic generation; Industrial research
ABSTRACT: In the context of the continuous evolution of the industrial internet, cybersecurity ranges play a crucial role in supporting the design and verification of industrial system protection mechanisms. Addressing the limitations of existing ranges in terms of construction complexity, traffic authenticity, and attack adaptability, this paper proposes the Large-language-model Assisted Security Range (LASR) framework. This framework combines the semantic modeling capabilities of large language models, hypergraph-driven network topology generation mechanisms, traffic synthesis methods using generative adversarial networks, and multi-agent collaboration mechanisms to build an intelligent simulation system with high scalability and adaptability. Each agent collaborates in attack modeling, intelligence analysis, defense response, and strategy optimization, achieving full-process modeling and strategy evolution of dynamic attack-defense processes. Taking the nuclear power industry as an example, this paper explores the application potential of this framework in complex industrial control systems, providing low-cost, high-performance theoretical support and system references for industrial cybersecurity research and protection technology verification. © 2025 World Scientific Publishing Company.

[R0187] year=2026 | venue=Expert Systems with Applications | type=Article
TITLE: PenExpert: A multi-agent hybrid LLM–expert system framework for autonomous penetration testing
KEYWORDS: Expert system; Large language models; Multi-agent system; Pentesting; Retrieval-augmented generation; Autonomous agents; Complex networks; Data mining; Decision making; Information retrieval; Intelligent agents; Multi agent systems; Network layers; Semantics; End to end; Language model; Large language model; Multi agent; Multiagent systems (MASs); Penetration testing; Pentesting; Retrieval-augmented generation; State management; System framework; Expert systems
ABSTRACT: Penetration testing (pentesting) is a critical technique for identifying and mitigating vulnerabilities in network systems, but traditional workflows rely heavily on manual effort, leading to lengthy assessment cycles and high costs. In recent years, while automated pentesting methods based on large language models (LLMs) have gained traction, they still face challenges in complex scenarios such as multi-host networks. These include linear task decomposition, insufficient domain knowledge coverage, and weak state management capabilities, leading to compromised testing efficiency and unreliable results. To address these issues, this paper introduces PenExpert–a multi-agent autonomous pentesting framework integrating LLMs and expert systems, designed to achieve end-to-end automated testing in black-box scenarios. PenExpert designs an expert system grounded in the MITRE ATT&CK knowledge base as its semantic foundation to enhance the penetration decision-making capabilities of LLMs. By constructing a specialized penetration payload repository and leveraging retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) technology, it enables precise payload invocation. Additionally, the framework introduces a structured penetration task state management component for accurate environmental awareness, enhanced state robustness, and recoverable task execution. Experiments demonstrate that PenExpert achieves 7%–123% relative improvement in subtask completion rates across different scenarios and baselines. To the best of our knowledge, it is the first framework to demonstrate end-to-end autonomous penetration in a five-layer network scenario, validating the framework’s effectiveness and scalability in complex penetration environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.

[R0188] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Conference on Smart Futuristic Technology, ICSFT 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AI-Based Asynchronous Alert Enrichment Framework for Scalable Security Operations Centers
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Alert Enrichment; Asynchronous Systems; Cybersecurity Analytics; Human-in-the-Loop Automation; Large Language Models (LLMs); Security Operations Center (SOC); Threat Intelligence; Artificial intelligence; Computation theory; Concurrency control; Intelligent agents; Network security; Security systems; Agentic AI; Alert enrichment; Asynchronous system; Cyber security; Cybersecurity analytic; Human-in-the-loop; Human-in-the-loop automation; Language model; Large language model; Security operation center; Threat intelligence; Semantics
ABSTRACT: Security Operations Centers (SOCs) face increasing operational pressure from growing alert volumes, diverse telemetry, and manual triage demands, while traditional SIEM and SOAR workflows - limited by static rules and sequential processing - struggle to scale. This paper presents an agentic, asynchronous alert-enrichment framework that reimagines enrichment as a parallel evidence-gathering and reasoning task performed by an autonomous agent integrating policy checks, multi-source asynchronous intelligence collection, LLM-based semantic analysis, and confidence-driven decision control with optional human escalation. A prototype built with modular connectors and an asynchronous execution model is evaluated under rising alert concurrency, demonstrating low overhead, stable latency, and reliable operation even under stress. Security analysis further highlights protections against adversarial inputs and unsafe automation. The results show that agentic AI can function as a practical, governance-aware accelerator for real-time SOC alert enrichment. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0189] year=2026 | venue=Electronics (Switzerland) | type=Article
TITLE: Security-Augmented Use Case Flow Refinement for LLM-Based Agentic Systems
KEYWORDS: LLM-based agentic systems; multi-agent systems; security requirements engineering; use case flow refinement; Computer software; Feedback; Graphic methods; Network security; Requirements engineering; Risk perception; Security systems; Language model; Large language model-based agentic system; Model-based OPC; Multiagent systems (MASs); Security analysis; Security requirements engineering; Software-systems; Use case flow; Use case flow refinement; Work-flows; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: Unlike conventional software systems and large language models (LLMs) evaluated in isolation, LLM-based agentic systems introduce a compositional, workflow-level attack surface. Existing security analysis approaches provide limited support for identifying security omissions implicit in functional use case flows and deriving corresponding flow refinements for direct incorporation into use case specifications. We formulate the Security-Augmented Use Case Flow Refinement Task and propose MA-SAFR, a multi-agent framework that coordinates Red Team, Blue Team, and Critic agents for threat anchoring, security-branch construction, and validation. EvoGraphRAG adapts relation-aware retrieval through validation feedback, while Risk-Specific Security Guidance (RSSG) provides risk- and role-specific constraints. To support systematic evaluation of this task, we construct SAFR-Bench from OWASP and MITRE ATLAS, comprising 157 cases and 235 source-grounded threat–refinement pairs. Across three generation models, MA-SAFR improves all three metrics on average compared with the strongest corresponding baselines; pipeline recall and pipeline precision increase by 63.44% and 54.96%, respectively. Controlled analyses show that RSSG and feedback-based graph evolution within gEvoGraphRAG improve downstream threat-to-defense conversion. Despite remaining challenges in implicit-threat identification, multi-threat coverage, and defense alignment, the results show that MA-SAFR can support the construction of valid security-augmented use case flows. © 2026 by the authors.

[R0190] year=2025 | venue=2025 International Conference on Signal Processing, Computer Networks and Communications, SPCNC 2025 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic RAG for Command Generation in Automated Penetration Testing
KEYWORDS: agent; llm; penetration testing; rag; security; Distributed computer systems; Information retrieval; Information retrieval systems; Intelligent agents; Knowledge based systems; Query processing; Search engines; Command generation; External knowledge; Knowledge gaps; Language model; Llm; Penetration testing; Rag; Reasoning process; Retrieval mechanisms; Security; Agents
ABSTRACT: Large language models (LLMs) have shown great potential in automated penetration testing (PT), but they need to rely on external knowledge to obtain precise output in complex command generation tasks. Although the traditional retrievalaugmented generation (RAG) can supplement external evidence to alleviate the hallucination problem, its static, single-round retrieval mechanism struggles to meet the dynamic knowledge needs of PT: the initial retrieval results may not cover the knowledge gaps generated during the reasoning process. To address it, this paper proposes a Reason-in-Document framework based on Agentic RAG, which transforms a single query into multi-step dynamic retrieval reasoning. Following the ReAct paradigm, the agent identifies knowledge gaps in the reasoning process, constructs targeted queries, and retrieves knowledge using the designated tools. By this mechanism, the system can gradually evolve the initial single query into a semantically progressive sub-query, enabling layer-by-layer knowledge refinement from conceptual-level understanding to implementation-level details.In this paper, a prototype system is implemented on the basis of PentestGPT, a structured knowledge base is constructed, and a variety of retrieval tool sets are designed. In the experiments of 22 real vulnerability scenarios, the Agentic RAG framework improves the accuracy of penetration command generation by 36% compared with the traditional RAG, which significantly enhances the knowledge utilization rate and the ability to generate complex commands. It provides a more efficient and accurate command generation mechanism for intelligent penetration testing. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0191] year=2026 | venue=International Conference of Young Specialists on Micro/Nanotechnologies and Electron Devices, EDM | type=Conference paper
TITLE: THAITI Verifiable Synthesis of Wazuh and Suricata Rules for Proactive Threat Hunting
KEYWORDS: detection rule synthesis; evidence memory; intermediate representations; investigation memory; knowledge graph; large language models; retrieval augmented generation; security operations center; Suricata; threat hunting; threat intelligence; validation; Wazuh; Computation theory; Computational methods; Embedded systems; Intelligent agents; Memory architecture; Network security; Security systems; Software prototyping; Detection rule synthesis; Detection rules; Evidence memory; Intermediate representations; Investigation memory; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Retrieval augmented generation; Security operation center; Suricata; Threat hunting; Threat intelligence; Validation; Wazuh; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Security operations centers increasingly rely on threat hunting to complement reactive alert handling and to turn investigative findings into detection rules ready for deployment. However, large language models used as analyst assistants may produce incomplete, outdated, or incorrect statements, and the resulting rules can be syntactically valid yet operationally unusable. This paper presents THAITI, a locally deployed multiagent prototype for threat hunting using AI agents and threat intelligence. THAITI combines persistent investigation memory with a verifiable rule synthesis loop for Wazuh and Suricata. The system records intermediate artifacts using versioned schemas, validates them automatically, and links every material claim in hypotheses and reports to extracted evidence such as events, documents, and knowledge graph elements. Instead of generating rules directly, THAITI produces constrained intermediate representations, compiles them deterministically into Wazuh and Suricata rules, and validates the rules on replayed telemetry and, when required, in an isolated laboratory environment. We describe the THAITI architecture, evidence retrieval strategies, and an evaluation methodology that emphasizes rule executability, false positive rate, and end to end traceability from conclusions to underlying evidence. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0192] year=2026 | venue=2026 9th International Conference on Intelligent Computing and Control Systems, ICICCS 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: SecureNet: A Next-Generation AI-Powered Cyber Defense System for Dynamic Attack Prevention
KEYWORDS: Adaptive Threat Scoring; AI-Driven Cybersecurity; Autonomous Cyber Defense Systems; BB84 Simulation; Cyberpunk Dashboard Interface; Deception-Based Defense; Emergency Lockdown Protocol; Honeypot Trap Files; Intrusion Detection; IP Geolocation Tracking; MirrorNet Simulation; Quantum-Inspired Cryptography; Real-Time Threat Monitoring; SecureNet Framework; Web-Based Security Architecture; Anomaly detection; Application programming interfaces (API); Computer architecture; Crime; Cybersecurity; Data privacy; Distributed computer systems; High level languages; Internet protocols; Locks (fasteners); Malware; Network security; Quantum cryptography; Websites; Adaptive threat scoring; AI-driven cybersecurity; Autonomous cybe defense system; Bb84 simulation; Cyber security; Cyber-defense; Cyberpunk dashboard interface; Deception-based defense; Defence systems; Emergency lockdown protocol; Geolocations; Honeypot trap file; Honeypots; Intrusion-Detection; IP geolocation tracking; Mirrornet simulation; Quantum-inspired cryptography; Real- time; Real-time threat monitoring; Securenet framework; Security Architecture; Web based; Web-based security architecture; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: As the Metaverse is rapidly evolving, the security framework of the system will need to evolve to protect users against more cyberattacks. The growing rise in the size of Large Language Models (LLMs) has amplified risks of email phishing attacks, ransomware hits, data breaches, and state-sponsored attacks that have become more advanced and require smarter and adaptive responses. To meet this requirement, different secure services are provided by the use of AI-based, web-based cybersecurity ecosystem, called SecureNet that combines threat-monitoring, anomaly-detection, geolocation of attack that allows tracking the attack origin, quantum inspired encryption, and deceptionbased defense mechanisms. It is based on cyberpunk-themed dashboards, the system is built on Python-Flask server code that uses dynamic HTML, CSS, and JavaScript elements to represent real-time cyber-attack conditions. The fundamental modules that incorporate the live scoring of threats, IP geolocation tracking, trap files identification, emergency lockdown measures, and MirrorNet-based simulation of threats that are effective to test the incorporation of modern cyber defense systems. It has an architecture based on a modular clientserver model that allows continuous updates, simulated API-based interaction of data and responsive UI interaction. System testing to validate the stable operation of all modules, visualizing threats, mapping of geolocation accurately, and simulated lockdown performance, and smooth running of quantum key distribution logic are all confirmed. Combining the tricks of deception and AI-powered analytics, as well as accessible and intuitive visualization, SecureNet offers a scalable and easy-to-learn environment to learn about cyber-attack behavior and defensive response flows. This project exemplifies the potential to design lightweight technologies into extensive, impactful, and real-time cybersecurity simulation that may be applied to develop academic learning, conduct prototyping, and, subsequently, expand to fully autonomous cyber defense infrastructures. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0193] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings - Annual Computer Security Applications Conference, ACSAC | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Clouseau: A Hierarchical Multi-Agent Approach for Autonomous Attack Investigation
KEYWORDS: Attack Investigation; Digital Forensics; Incident Response; Large Language Models; Security Applications of Generative AI; Computational methods; Computer crime; Electronic crime countermeasures; Heuristic methods; Labeled data; Learning systems; Multi agent systems; Network security; 'current; Attack investigation; Attacks scenarios; Cyber-attacks; Incident response; Language model; Large language model; Multi-agent approach; Security application; Security application of generative AI; Digital forensics
ABSTRACT: Cyberattack investigations are crucial for understanding the Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures of adversaries, but they face increasing challenges due to the complexity, scale, and volume of modern cyber incidents. Current approaches, such as heuristic-based and learning-based methods, struggle with scalability and reliance on labeled data, leading to difficulties in adapting to new threats. In this paper, we present Clouseau,a hierarchical multi-agent approach that leverages the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) to autonomously investigate cyberattacks from a single Point-Of-Interest while requiring neither prior training nor predefined heuristics. We evaluated Clouseauon 21 diverse attack scenarios, including complex cases from DARPA's OpTC engagements and Advanced Persistent Threat (APT) attack scenarios from the ATLAS dataset. In single-host settings, Clouseauachieves an average F1 score of 99.78%, surpassing strong baselines by more than 33%. To demonstrate its broad applicability, we tested Clouseau with both proprietary and open-weight LLMs, achieving strong performance in both cases, thereby enabling deployment in private environments where access to proprietary models is restricted. © 2025 IEEE.

[R0194] year=2026 | venue=2026 IEEE International Conference on Automatic Control and Intelligent Systems, I2CACIS 2026 - Conference Proceedings | type=Conference paper
TITLE: GenAI vs Traditional AI: A Comparative Framework Across Five Domains
KEYWORDS: Agentic AI; Artificial Intelligence; Discriminative AI; Edge-Cloud Computing; Generative AI; Large Language Models; Anomaly detection; Architecture; Computer architecture; Decision support systems; Edge computing; Medical computing; Network architecture; Probability distributions; Agentic artificial intelligence; Cloud-computing; Conditional probabilities; Discriminative artificial intelligence; Discriminative models; Edge clouds; Edge-cloud computing; Generative artificial intelligence; Language model; Large language model; Artificial intelligence
ABSTRACT: Artificial intelligence (AI) is undergoing a major transition from traditional discriminative models which estimate conditional probabilities to classify data and predict outcomes to Generative AI (GenAI). GenAI instead approximates joint probability distributions to synthesize novel, high-fidelity artifacts across multi-modal domains such as text, code, and imagery This work presents a comparative examination of the primary architectures underlying this transition, namely Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), Transformer architectures, and diffusion-based models. It further examines how architectural distinctions influence practical deployment across sectors. In cybersecurity, discriminative systems remain effective for anomaly detection, whereas generative models strengthen defense by simulating zero-day attack scenarios. In healthcare, generative techniques support synthetic data generation and clinical decision assistance. Despite these capabilities, generative systems introduce significant concerns, including inherited dataset bias, substantial computational demand, and hallucinated responses lacking factual grounding. The paper concludes by describing the movement toward Agentic AI autonomous entities capable of sequential reasoning and external tool utilization relying on distributed edge cloud architectures. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0195] year=2025 | venue=Proceedings of Machine Learning Research | type=Conference paper
TITLE: AdvAgent: Controllable Blackbox Red-teaming on Web Agents
KEYWORDS: Adversarial machine learning; Autonomous agents; Computational methods; Decision making; Network security; Autonomous decision; Black boxes; Complex task; Decisions makings; Foundation models; Model-based OPC; Red teaming; Reinforcement learnings; Security risks; Web Agents; Agents
ABSTRACT: Foundation model-based agents are increasingly used to automate complex tasks, enhancing efficiency and productivity. However, their access to sensitive resources and autonomous decision-making also introduce significant security risks, where successful attacks could lead to severe consequences. To systematically uncover these vulnerabilities, we propose AdvAgent, a blackbox red-teaming framework for attacking web agents. Unlike existing approaches, AdvAgent employs a reinforcement learning-based pipeline to train an adversarial prompter model that optimizes adversarial prompts using feedback from the black-box agent. With careful attack design, these prompts effectively exploit agent weaknesses while maintaining stealthiness and controllability. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that AdvAgent achieves high success rates against state-of-the-art GPT-4-based web agents across diverse web tasks. Furthermore, we find that existing prompt-based defenses provide only limited protection, leaving agents vulnerable to our framework. These findings highlight critical vulnerabilities in current web agents and emphasize the urgent need for stronger defense mechanisms. We release our code at https://ai-secure.github.io/AdvAgent/. © 2025 by the author(s).

[R0196] year=2025 | venue=International Conference on Information Systems Security and Privacy | type=Conference paper
TITLE: GAI-Driven Offensive Cybersecurity: Transforming Pentesting for Proactive Defence
KEYWORDS: Dynamic Payload Generation; GAI; Large Language Models; Offensive Cyber security; Pentesting; Proactive Cyber security Defence; SGPT; Vulnerability Assessment
ABSTRACT: Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI), particularly Large Language Models (LLMs) like Shell GPT (SGPT), offers transformative potential in automating penetration testing (pentesting) tasks, enabling organizations to strengthen their cyber security defenses. This paper discusses the integration of GAI into pentesting workflows, covering phases such as reconnaissance, exploitation, and post-exploitation. GAI reduces manual effort by automating key tasks, such as dynamic payload generation and adaptive exploitation, which in turn accelerates the assessments and enhances the accuracy of vulnerability detection. Our case study will show how GAI-driven automation improves the efficiency of pentesting while reducing costs, thus making advanced security assessments available to organizations of all sizes. GAI integration will also overcome the pitfalls of traditional approaches that are intensive and expensive, hence putting small-scale organizations at risk. Application of GAI in virtualized environments provides a means to construct dynamic synthetic test beds that further improve assessment robustness. These results prove that GAI can revolutionize pentesting into a scalable, adaptive, and cost effective process. It concludes by emphasizing the role of GAI in democratizing proactive cyber security measures, making comprehensive security testing possible even for resource- constrained organizations. © 2025 by SCITEPRESS-Science and Technology Publications, Lda.


